This is one of the most important parts of the product.

The phone must control the actual computer, not merely display a cursor inside the receiver window.

Implement a native input adapter for each supported desktop platform.

## 8.1 Mouse operations

Implement:

- Relative pointer movement.
- Optional absolute pointer movement where supported.
- Left click.
- Right click.
- Middle click.
- Double click.
- Press and hold.
- Drag and drop.
- Two-finger scrolling.
- Horizontal scrolling where supported.

The receiver must convert the common input-event format into the correct native platform events.

## 8.2 Keyboard operations

Implement:

- Basic keyboard input.
- Modifier keys.
- Key combinations.
- Escape.
- Enter.
- Backspace.
- Tab.
- Arrow keys.
- Function keys where supported.
- Common shortcuts.

Keyboard support must be explicitly capability-negotiated.

Do not assume the same keyboard layout or key mapping across operating systems.

Handle Unicode text separately from physical key events.

Document platform-specific limitations.

## 8.3 Input safety

Implement a release-all mechanism.

When any of the following occurs:

- Connection loss.
- Session expiry.
- Receiver shutdown.
- Mobile application crash.
- Input protocol error.
- Watchdog timeout.

The receiver must release all simulated pressed buttons and keys.

Implement a watchdog that periodically verifies valid session activity.

Do not allow a malformed or unauthenticated event to trigger system input.

